{\*\ftnsep\chftnsep}\pgndec\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Plot/Intro}{
 [00:00:03\u8211\'9600:00:36]\line Welcome to The Lighter Side of the Spectrum, the podcast that celebrates the multiple hats we wear and the juggling acts we perform in life. Whether you're parenting kids and teens on the autism spectrum, navigating the ever-evolving healthcare industry, or building a more inclusive and impactful world, this is your space to find connection, inspiration, and growth. Together, we'll share stories, tackle challenges, and explore how innovation and inclusion create better outcomes for everyone. Because no matter the spectrum, there's always a lighter side. Now here's your host, David Kaufer.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:00:36\u8211\'9600:00:49]\line Hello again, everybody. This is David Kaufer. Welcome back to The Lighter Side of the Spectrum, where we explore the people, ideas, and stone\u8212\'97}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David and Stone Kaufer}{
 [00:00:49\u8211\'9600:00:49]\line Hi. Hi. How are you? Good, good. Happy, happy. Okay, okay.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:00:49\u8211\'9600:02:33]\line And conversations that help us better understand autism communication and what it really means to support human potential. You know, one of the things I've certainly learned over the last few years is that so many of the assumptions that we make about people turn out to be wrong. We've learned that what Looks like a lack of understanding may actually be a motor planning challenge. We've also learned that what looks like a behavioral issue may be sensory overload. What appears to be an inability to communicate may simply be a person who hasn't been given the right way to communicate.\line Today's conversation is going to ask us to consider another assumption. What if vision isn't just about seeing clearly? You know, most of us think of vision as reading, reading the eye chart at your doctor's office, trying to see if you have 20/20 vision. You know, if you're good, you're good to go. But what if your eyes are healthy, yet your brain isn't using them together in the way it's meant to be?\line What if the issue isn't how well you see, but how you see? And that's the fascinating world we're going to step into today. And my guest today is Denise Allen, host of Healing Our Sight podcast and a passionate advocate for helping people better understand binocular vision and the remarkable adaptability of the human brain. Denise spent most of her life with strabomide\u8212\'97 strabomys\u8212\'97 I knew I was gonna screw it up. Well, help me, Denise.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:02:33\u8211\'9600:02:34]\line Strabismus.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:02:34\u8211\'9600:03:35]\line Strabismus. I even practiced it. And without stereoscopic or 3D vision, like many people, She assumed that was simply the way her brain worked. But as an adult, she discovered that our visual system may be far more adaptable than many of us realize. And that journey led her into the worlds of vision therapy, neuroplasticity, developmental optometry, and neuroscience.\line Along the way, she's had conversations with leading experts while helping parents, patients, and professionals better understand conditions. And related to vision-related challenges. So this isn't about autism specifically, but we certainly see it within autism. And I think it's really going to be a fascinating conversation because vision, sensory processing, motor planning, communication, they're all parts of an incredibly complex system. So Denise, with all that being said, thanks for joining me today.\line I'm really looking forward to this conversation.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:03:35\u8211\'9600:03:37]\line Thanks for having me.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:03:37\u8211\'9600:03:46]\line Yeah. So why don't you tell us a little bit about your experience? What, what, tell us about what is it like to grow up with this condition?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:03:46\u8211\'9600:03:53]\line Well, you know, I didn't know anything different, so I thought I was pretty normal.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:03:54\u8211\'9600:03:54]\line Okay.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:03:55\u8211\'9600:04:17]\line But I did have struggles with coordination, being very clumsy, not very good at catching Or, you know, balls or anything, any moving object coming at me was difficult to gauge where it was and catch it. So I was really bad at ballgames. So I was usually chosen last, you know, that kind of thing.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:04:17\u8211\'9600:04:18]\line Right.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:04:18\u8211\'9600:04:28]\line That posed a problem, of course. My knees were always scraped up, you know, I fell down. I just thought I was a clumsy kid, you know.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:04:28\u8211\'9600:04:34]\line Yeah. So you just thought you were clumsy. I mean, right? Like, You hear about that?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:04:34\u8211\'9600:05:13]\line So my mom put me in dance class. That was supposed to help. Didn't, didn't love it that much. No, no, it did help. And then I actually did gymnastics in junior high, which I shouldn't have probably been able to do very well. I was too tall for it. Wow. But yeah, that's challenging. It doesn't stop someone from doing something that they want to do. My brother, who had the same condition, only his showed up when he was a baby, he became a basketball player. He played college basketball, which also shouldn't really be very easy. But he was passionate about basketball.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:05:13\u8211\'9600:05:14]\line Wow.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:05:14\u8211\'9600:05:23]\line So he overcame that. So I think we don't have to discount anything, right? But the thing that I found is that it can be\u8212\'97}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:05:25\u8211\'9600:05:46]\line That's just\u8212\'97 yep, Stone. Stone's very excited today. So again, he's making, making lots of appearances. He's getting, getting ready to head out with his brother. But that's amazing though. I mean, that he was able to play basketball, because again, why you\u8212\'97 I mean, we'll back up a little bit and maybe kind of talk about, um, strabismus. Is that\u8212\'97 did I get it better?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:05:46\u8211\'9600:05:47]\line Yeah.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:05:47\u8211\'9600:05:56]\line Okay. Um, What is it, you know, and how is it defined and how does it impact people?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:05:56\u8211\'9600:06:19]\line Yeah, so strabismus is when the eyes don't focus together. And the easy explanation is just that they're fighting with each other all the time. And in order to compensate for that, one of the eyes will shut off. And when you look at someone, Often that eye is pointing in another direction.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:06:20\u8211\'9600:06:20]\line Okay.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:06:20\u8211\'9600:06:37]\line Because it's getting out of the way of the other eye that's the strong eye. So you'll see kids with an eye turn, right? Crossed eyes, however you want to term it, right? It can be turning in or it can be turning out. People will call it a lazy eye.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:06:37\u8211\'9600:06:41]\line Yeah, you hear about that. Okay.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:06:41\u8211\'9600:07:03]\line So that's kind of And so it's like 5% of the population that have an actual strabismus like this, of the normal population. If you're looking at the autistic spectrum, then it's totally different. And that's just strabismus. That's not other vision problems like inability to track or\u8212\'97}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:07:03\u8211\'9600:07:05]\line Right.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:07:05\u8211\'9600:07:13]\line Other focusing types of issues like that. You know, so my Mine is kind of a subset, but it's a much bigger problem than that.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:07:13\u8211\'9600:07:42]\line Is this something that we're just, like so many other things, we're just learning more about? I mean, is there just\u8212\'97 is it one of those we're just getting more awareness? Because growing up, I mean, what was\u8212\'97 like you said, you were just kind of considered maybe a little clumsy, or you went in for traditional eye exams, right? Like, you know, was it just\u8212\'97 what did the eye doctors see or say, you know? And what did it take for you to finally Yeah, that is a great question.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:07:42\u8211\'9600:08:02]\line And I, and I think that it's always been a problem. I just feel like the fact that we can do something about it hasn't been very obvious. So when I\u8212\'97 my brother, it was obvious at birth. He went into the doctor, they did surgery on his eyes because it was pointing way in, you know.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:08:02\u8211\'9600:08:06]\line So it was so obvious that like, they're like, okay, we got to fix this. I mean\u8212\'97}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:08:06\u8211\'9600:08:20]\line Exactly. Yeah. And the traditional solution When you have this kind of an eye turn is the ophthalmologist will say, well, we're going to do surgery. They'll do 2 or 3 surgeries. Often it takes more than one. My brother had 3 surgeries.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:08:20\u8211\'9600:08:20]\line Oh, wow.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:08:20\u8211\'9600:09:07]\line Yeah. 2 when he was a baby and one when he was like 7 to kind of tune it up. So it's kind of a lot of surgery for a young child. It doesn't teach the eyes how to work together though, to have surgery. So even though my brother's eyes were fairly straight, He didn't learn how to see depth.\line My problem didn't surface until I was almost 3. So it's a different type of strabismus. There's infantile, like my brother, and then there's a compensatory type of strabismus. And it usually means that you have either farsighted or nearsightedness, and your eyes are not working together and they're compensating. And they put glasses on the child like they did me, and the eyes look pretty straight.\line And the doctor says, okay, you're good to go.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:09:09\u8211\'9600:09:11]\line Just because your eyes were straight?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:09:11\u8211\'9600:09:45]\line Exactly. And I remember as a child going in for my yearly exam, and I was about 10. And they did this test where you look in the big machine. And on one side, there's a note, and on the other side, there's the 5 lines for the music staff. And they said, can you tell us what line or space that note's on. And I said, well, I see the notes over here and I see the lines over here. Do you want me to guess? And I had been guessing until then.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:09:45\u8211\'9600:09:46]\line Oh, really?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
Denise Allen}{
 [00:09:46\u8211\'9600:09:58]\line Something just clicked. And I thought\u8212\'97 and I was annoyed because I'd had a traumatic event on the way to the office. And so, I think I was probably a little more sassy than usual, you know?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\kerning1\cf0\rtlch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af8\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\ql\ltrpar{\b
David Kaufer}{
 [00:09:58\u8211\'9600:10:00]\line Yeah, a little edgy, a little edgy 10-year-old.}
